+ * SMP snapshots: each held CPU drains its per-cpu page lists before it
+ * checks in (its seq-cst check-in orders that before CPU 0's claim and
+ * free-page walk; CPU 0 cannot drain them for it). CPU 0 calls
+ * wasm_snapshot_point() outside its irqentry_enter(), so every CPU's idle
+ * task is in the same state there; kwparkd is bound to CPU 0, so a failure
+ * is reported there too. A restore enters kw_resume on CPU 0 alone, which
+ * starts the others again (kw_secondary_resume): each CPU's idle task
+ * leaves the saved idle state on its fresh stack (kw_park_restart_idle()),
+ * and kwparkd waits for every CPU before it does its restore work and
+ * resumes the tasks, each where it is held. The CPU count must be the
+ * snapshot's (the host checks it).
